course: dictionary
lesson: 6803
title: Organise: plan it or put it in order
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Organise is a verb, so it is something you do. It can mean plan and arrange something, put things in order, or get people working together. «Sam wants to organise a party for his birthday.» Here Sam is going to plan the party and arrange it.
explain: The words around organise show you which meaning fits. «Mia organised her pencil case by colour.» Nothing is being planned here, so Mia has put her things in order. When a class does a project together, organise means getting people to work together.
explain: Check your guess by putting it into the sentence. If your meaning still makes sense in place of the word, you are probably right. Pupils who know the first meaning can use it to understand the other two.
fact: Organise means to plan and arrange something, such as a party, a trip or a game.
fact: To organise things is to put them in order, like your books or your pencil case.
fact: «A non-chronological report organises facts by topic, not by time.» Here it means it puts facts into groups.
fact: Americans spell it organize, with a z, but the meaning is the same.
fact: The past tense is organised: «The Persian Empire was well organised and fairly tolerant for its time.»
exercise: Sam wants to organise a birthday party. What does organise mean here? | To put his pencil case in order | To make the room dark | *To plan and arrange it | To sing loudly
exercise: «The Persian Empire was well organised and fairly tolerant for its time.» What does organised mean here? | Very small and hard to find | Painted in bright colours | *Well planned and run in good order | Frightened and running away
exercise: Find and copy the word that means put things in order: «Mia organised her pencil case by colour.» | Mia | colour | pencil | *organised
test: «A non-chronological report organises facts by topic, not by time.» Organises here means | Shouts the facts loudly | *Puts facts into groups or order | Tears up the facts | Tells a story in time order
test: What is the past tense of organise? | *organised | organiser | organising | organises
exam: Which sentence uses organise to mean get people to work together? | The sun will organise the clouds tomorrow | My dog likes to organise his bed | I organise my books by size on the shelf | *Our class will organise a bake sale to raise money
